---
# clang-tidy for Desert Engine. Pinned to LLVM 18, the same release the clang-format gate stands on:
# an analyser whose verdict depends on which version happens to be installed is the defect the
# formatter pin closed on 2026-09-08, and reopening it here would cost the same three red runs.
# Run it with scripts/CI/CheckTidy.sh, which enforces the version and builds the compiler database.
#
# The check list below started as the owner's list from his backend project. What changed and why is
# recorded at the bottom of this file — every deviation is named, because a check silently dropped is
# indistinguishable from a check nobody noticed was missing.
Checks: '-*,google-*,boost-*,readability-*,-readability-named-parameter,misc-*,linuxkernel-*,performance-*,-performance-inefficient-string-concatenation,modernize-*,-modernize-use-trailing-return-type,-modernize-pass-by-value,cppcoreguidelines-*,-cppcoreguidelines-avoid-non-const-global-variables,concurrency-*,bugprone-*,-cppcoreguidelines-special-member-functions,-misc-non-private-member-variables-in-classes,-misc-definitions-in-headers,-readability-redundant-member-init,-google-global-names-in-headers,-google-build-using-namespace,-cppcoreguidelines-avoid-const-or-ref-data-members,-cppcoreguidelines-macro-usage,clang-diagnostic-*,-clang-diagnostic-gnu-zero-variadic-macro-arguments,-cppcoreguidelines-avoid-c-arrays,-cppcoreguidelines-non-private-member-variables-in-classes,-google-readability-avoid-underscore-in-googletest-name,-bugprone-reserved-identifier,-bugprone-macro-parentheses,-google-build-namespaces,-performance-enum-size,-google-explicit-constructor,-bugprone-easily-swappable-parameters,-boost-use-ranges,-misc-use-internal-linkage,-readability-avoid-const-params-in-decls,-cppcoreguidelines-missing-std-forward,-misc-unused-using-decls,-performance-avoid-endl,-misc-use-anonymous-namespace'

# HEADERS ARE ANALYSED, and this is the one setting whose old value silently narrowed the tool's reach.
# `HeaderFilterRegex: ''` matches nothing, so clang-tidy reports diagnostics from the .cpp only.
# Measured over the whole workspace (565 translation units, llvm 18.1.8): 8 258 of the 67 406 DISTINCT
# source locations the check set finds are in our own .hpp/.h — 12.3 % of the defects, and 89 % of the
# printed lines (648 697 of 727 689), because a header is re-reported by every unit that includes it.
# The 12.3 % is the number that matters: those are findings that an empty filter makes UNREACHABLE, and
# this engine puts a large share of its logic in headers (every CRTP widget, every template container,
# every inline getter).
#
# The regex names OUR source roots rather than excluding ThirdParty, because llvm::Regex is POSIX ERE
# with no negative lookahead and there are two third-party roots (ThirdParty/ and Editor/ThirdParty/).
# Listing where our code lives is also the version that does not need editing when a submodule is
# added: a new dependency lands under a ThirdParty/ directory, which no alternative below matches.
#
# The path segments are spelled `[A-Za-z0-9_+-]+`, which cannot match `..`, and THAT is what keeps
# third-party headers out. clang reports a header by the path it resolved, so `#include
# <DesertShared/ResultStr.hpp>` from Desert/Common comes back as
# `Desert/Common/Source/Common/Core/../../../../../ThirdParty/desert-shared/Include/...` — a string
# that begins inside our tree and ends in somebody else's. A regex that merely starts at our roots
# lets every one of those through.
HeaderFilterRegex: '(Desert/(Desert|Common|Tests)|Editor/Source|Runtime/Source|Tools/[A-Za-z]+/Source)/([A-Za-z0-9_+-]+/)*[A-Za-z0-9_+-]+\.(hpp|h)$'

# KEPT AS THE OWNER WROTE IT. This is what gives the gate an exit code: clang-tidy returns 0 over a
# wall of warnings, and both run-clang-tidy and clang-tidy-diff.py report the maximum child exit
# code, so without this a gate reading the return value is green no matter what it found. Measured on
# JobSystem.cpp: 62 diagnostics either way, exit 0 without it and exit 1 with it.
# It does NOT truncate the listing — that was the suspicion, and it is wrong: clang-tidy re-maps
# severity in its own diagnostic consumer, it does not hand clang -Werror, so nothing stops early and
# the same 62 lines are printed. Leaving it here rather than in the script also means an editor or a
# bare `clang-tidy Foo.cpp` agrees with CI about whether the file passes.
WarningsAsErrors: '*'
FormatStyle: none
CheckOptions:
  - key:             llvm-else-after-return.WarnOnConditionVariables
    value:           'false'
  - key:             modernize-loop-convert.MinConfidence
    value:           reasonable
  - key:             modernize-replace-auto-ptr.IncludeStyle
    value:           llvm
  - key:             google-readability-namespace-comments.ShortNamespaceLines
    value:           '10'
  - key:             google-readability-braces-around-statements.ShortStatementLines
    value:           '1'
  - key:             cppcoreguidelines-non-private-member-variables-in-classes.IgnoreClassesWithAllMemberVariablesBeingPublic
    value:           'true'
  - key:             google-readability-namespace-comments.SpacesBeforeComments
    value:           '2'
  - key:             modernize-loop-convert.MaxCopySize
    value:           '16'
  - key:             modernize-pass-by-value.IncludeStyle
    value:           llvm
  - key:             modernize-use-nullptr.NullMacros
    value:           'NULL'
  - key:             llvm-qualified-auto.AddConstToQualified
    value:           'false'
  - key:             modernize-loop-convert.NamingStyle
    value:           CamelCase
  - key:             llvm-else-after-return.WarnOnUnfixable
    value:           'false'
  - key:             google-readability-function-size.StatementThreshold
    value:           '500'
  # KEPT AT THE OWNER'S 19, AND THE SUSPICION THAT IT IS TOO LOW FOR A RENDERER WAS MEASURED AND IS
  # NOT WHAT THE NUMBERS SAY. Over the workspace it fires 854 times (282 of them outside the tests).
  # Raising it barely helps and costs exactly the functions worth hearing about: 25 leaves 625, 30
  # leaves 473, 50 leaves 172, 100 leaves 44. The median COMPLEXITY of a firing function is 32 and the
  # worst is 487 (Editor/.../PropertyEditorBuilder.cpp DrawField) — so the population is not "slightly
  # over a strict line", it is a long tail, and any threshold that quietened it would have to sit
  # above 100, i.e. above every function anyone would call complicated.
  # It is also nearly free under a changed-lines gate: the diagnostic is reported at the function's
  # DECLARATION line, so editing a statement in the middle of a 487-complexity function does not
  # produce it. It fires when you write a new function that is already too complex, or when you touch
  # the signature of one that is — which is exactly when it is worth being told.
  - key:             readability-function-cognitive-complexity.Threshold
    value:           '19'
...

# WHAT THE OWNER'S CONFIG DID WHEN IT WAS RUN, AND WHY NONE OF IT HAD EVER RUN.
#
# The file arrived from the owner's backend project and had been in this repository since 27 July
# with nothing invoking it. Every statement below was produced by running clang-tidy 18.1.8 against
# the file itself (`--dump-config`, `--list-checks`), not by reading it.
#
# It carried SIX defects, and the first one is the reason the other five were invisible: `Checks:`
# was indented five spaces under the `---`, so clang-tidy read the check list and SILENTLY DISCARDED
# every key after it. `--dump-config` on the original reports `WarningsAsErrors: ''` where the file
# says `'*'`, and `User: danya100kg` — this machine's login — where the file says `altsybeev`. The
# other four are all FATAL, and the discard is what hid them: de-indent that one key and clang-tidy
# refuses the file outright, one error at a time, in this order:
#
#   1. `  value: 'L;LL;LU;LLU'` under `cert-dcl16-c.NewSuffixes` is indented two spaces where every
#      other value is indented four → `error: Unexpected token. Expected Block Entry or Block End.`
#   2. `clang-tidy.extra-args` with a YAML LIST value → `error: unexpected scalar`. A CheckOption
#      value must be a scalar; extra-args is a COMMAND-LINE flag and not a check option at all, so
#      `-Wno-enum-constexpr-conversion` was never applied. It is not needed either: this tree
#      produces zero clang-diagnostic-enum-constexpr-conversion. Should one appear, the flag belongs
#      in CheckTidy.sh's -extra-arg list.
#   3. `AnalyzeTemporaryDtors: false` → `error: unknown key 'AnalyzeTemporaryDtors'`. The option was
#      deleted from clang-tidy in LLVM 16. It is NOT ignored — it rejects the whole file.
#   4. `Extra`, a bare key on its own line left over from an edit → `error: unknown key 'Extra'`.
#      Also fatal, for the same reason.
#
# `User: altsybeev` is the sixth and the only non-fatal one: with the file parsing it DOES take
# effect (`--dump-config` prints altsybeev), feeding google-readability-todo another project's author
# name. Removed.
#
# So the honest summary is not "some of the config was ineffective". The config as written cannot be
# loaded at all, and the only reason clang-tidy accepted it is a YAML indentation slip that threw
# four fatal errors away along with every setting they sat next to.
#
# ALSO DELETED: a byte-identical second copy at Desert/.clang-tidy, which shadowed this one for the
# whole engine, Common and every test — 735 of the 912 database entries, 440 of the 565 distinct
# translation units. The files did not differ, so
# there was nothing to merge.
#
# ALSO DELETED: every `cert-*` option (NewSuffixes, CheckedFunctions,
# DiagnoseSignedUnsignedCharComparisons, WarnOnlyIfThisHasSuspiciousField). `cert-*` is not in
# `Checks` and `--list-checks` enables zero of them, so all four configured checks that never ran.
# The group stays OFF: its members that matter here are aliases of bugprone-* and cppcoreguidelines-*
# checks already enabled, and enabling both spellings double-reports every finding.
#
# WHAT WAS KEPT DESPITE LOOKING WRONG:
#  * `- cppcoreguidelines-non-private-member-variables-in-classes` had a space after the minus, which
#    looks like a glob that disables nothing. It is not: clang-tidy trims each glob. Measured with
#    `--list-checks` — the check is absent with the space, absent without it, and present when the
#    line is removed. The space is gone above for readability only; this note exists so nobody
#    "fixes" it a second time.
#  * `WarningsAsErrors: '*'` is kept exactly as the owner wrote it, because it is what gives the gate
#    an exit code at all: clang-tidy returns 0 over a wall of warnings, and both run-clang-tidy and
#    clang-tidy-diff.py report the maximum child exit code. It does NOT truncate the listing — that
#    was the suspicion and it is wrong; clang-tidy re-maps severity in its own diagnostic consumer
#    rather than handing clang -Werror, so the same diagnostics print either way. Keeping it in the
#    config rather than in the script also means an editor, or a bare `clang-tidy Foo.cpp`, agrees
#    with CI about whether a file passes.
